    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec required rejecting a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400. I added a single new, self-contained advice class:\n\n**`OwnerFutureRegistrationDateAdvice.java`** \u2014 a `RequestBodyAdviceAdapter` that reads the supplied `registrationDate` off the incoming `OwnerFieldsDto`, and if it's after `LocalDate.now()`, throws a small local exception mapped to `400 Bad Request` via an `@ExceptionHandler`.\n\n## Why this shape\n\nThe codebase already establishes a pattern of one tiny `@ControllerAdvice` per registration-date business rule (`OwnerRegistrationDateAdvice`, `OwnerDailyCreationLimitAdvice`). I followed it rather than editing the controller, service, or an existing handler \u2014 so **structural impact is minimal**: I touched no existing function (zero `changed_lines` on any existing method, so no WMC/CC cost), and the only file added is a new class whose own methods each have trivial complexity and no surrounding class context to hold.\n\nThe future-date check is also order-independent from the sibling `OwnerRegistrationDateAdvice` (which defaults nulls to today and rolls weekends forward): a future date stays future after any such adjustment, and a defaulted null becomes today (not after now), so correctness holds regardless of advice execution order.\n\nThe acceptance test `errorRejectsFutureDate` (posts `registrationDate: 2999-01-01`, expects `isBadRequest`) passes.",
